WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.104 --> 00:00:01.736
Imagine it's 2028.

2
00:00:02.005 --> 00:00:04.092
A startup launches with one employee.

3
00:00:04.362 --> 00:00:04.782
You.

4
00:00:05.052 --> 00:00:05.746
No team.

5
00:00:06.016 --> 00:00:06.827
No office.

6
00:00:07.096 --> 00:00:09.340
Just a laptop, a strong idea,

7
00:00:09.492 --> 00:00:12.334
and an army of AI agents working while you sleep.

8
00:00:12.617 --> 00:00:15.043
They answer support tickets, write the code,

9
00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:17.790
chase invoices, and ship the updates.

10
00:00:18.073 --> 00:00:20.720
By morning, your company has done a week of work.

11
00:00:20.990 --> 00:00:22.946
At first, it feels like magic.

12
00:00:23.216 --> 00:00:24.613
Then it feels like leverage.

13
00:00:24.896 --> 00:00:27.295
One person, doing what used to take fifty.

14
00:00:27.565 --> 00:00:30.414
A solo founder with a clear system can now ship

15
00:00:30.534 --> 00:00:31.814
like a team of twenty,

16
00:00:31.979 --> 00:00:34.874
and pivot before a committee finishes its first meeting.

17
00:00:35.156 --> 00:00:36.566
The tools are already here.

18
00:00:36.836 --> 00:00:39.014
The agents are getting better every month.

19
00:00:39.297 --> 00:00:41.267
The cost of starting is collapsing.

20
00:00:41.536 --> 00:00:43.624
And the old advantage, headcount,

21
00:00:43.906 --> 00:00:45.473
is becoming a liability.

22
00:00:45.755 --> 00:00:47.426
That doesn't make people worthless.

23
00:00:47.695 --> 00:00:50.668
It makes coordination cheap, and judgment scarce.

24
00:00:50.951 --> 00:00:53.116
But here's the part nobody puts in the demo.

25
00:00:53.385 --> 00:00:55.811
Automation can multiply a good decision.

26
00:00:56.081 --> 00:00:58.038
It can also multiply a bad one,

27
00:00:58.255 --> 00:00:59.991
faster than anyone can catch it.

28
00:01:00.273 --> 00:01:04.431
One careless prompt can become a thousand bad emails, or a headline.

29
00:01:04.701 --> 00:01:07.321
So the skill that matters isn't typing prompts.

30
00:01:07.591 --> 00:01:10.551
It's knowing which decisions should never be delegated.

31
00:01:10.820 --> 00:01:12.491
The one-person company is coming.

32
00:01:12.773 --> 00:01:15.303
The question is not whether you can run it alone.

33
00:01:15.573 --> 00:01:17.777
It's whether you'll know what still needs a human.

34
00:01:18.047 --> 00:01:19.639
Not because you can do everything.

35
00:01:19.909 --> 00:01:21.566
Because you know what not to automate.

36
00:01:21.849 --> 00:01:23.337
That's the future worth building.
